    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/herman-willem-daendels"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herman-willem-daendels.jpg" alt="Herman Willem Daendels" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/herman-willem-daendels">Herman Willem Daendels</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Posthuum_portret_van_Herman_Willem_Daendels_(1762-1818)._Gouverneur-generaal_(1808-10)_Rijksmuseum_SK-A-3790.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Raden Sarief Bastaman Saleh, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> were created when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> state took over the Asian possessions of the bankrupt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> (VOC), whose charter expired at the end of 1799. On 1 January 1800 its territories, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a> and scattered trading posts, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>’s wars soon reached the colony. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herman-willem-daendels">Herman Willem Daendels</a>, appointed governor-general by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-bonaparte">Louis Bonaparte</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-holland">Kingdom of Holland</a>, arrived in 1808, centralised the administration of Java, curbed the Javanese courts and built the Great Post Road along the north coast with forced labour. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> was annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1810, a British expedition took Java in 1811, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamford-raffles">Thomas Stamford Raffles</a> governed it as lieutenant-governor until 1816, introducing a land rent tax. Under the Anglo-Dutch Convention of 1814 the colony was returned, and the Dutch resumed control in 1816. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a> then divided the region into spheres, leaving the archipelago south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> to the Netherlands. Dutch authority remained limited outside Java, and on Java itself it was challenged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diponegoro">Prince Diponegoro</a> of Yogyakarta, whose rebellion of 1825–1830, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java-war">Java War</a>, cost perhaps 200,000 Javanese lives before he was arrested during negotiations and exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manado">Manado</a> and later Makassar.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cultivation-system"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cultivation-system.jpg" alt="Cultivation System" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cultivation-system">Cultivation System</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolaes_Visscher_Indiae_Orientalis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nicolaes Visscher II, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The colony’s economy was shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultivation-system">Cultivation System</a> (<em>cultuurstelsel</em>) introduced by Governor-General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-van-den-bosch">Johannes van den Bosch</a> in 1830. Javanese villages were required to devote part of their land or labour to export crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>, delivered to the government at fixed prices and shipped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/netherlands-trading-society">Netherlands Trading Society</a>. The surplus remitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> paid off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> debts and financed railways at home, but it also contributed to famines in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in the 1840s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multatuli">Eduard Douwes Dekker</a>’s novel <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-havelaar">Max Havelaar</a></em> (1860), published under the name Multatuli, attacked the system, and the Agrarian Law of 1870 opened the colony to private plantations. Tobacco estates in Deli on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, sugar mills on Java, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangka">Bangka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belitung">Belitung</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and, from the 1890s, oil drilled by the Royal Dutch company made the Indies one of the world’s most valuable colonies. Society was divided by law into Europeans, ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/foreign-orientals">Foreign Orientals</a>’, mainly Chinese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, and ‘Natives’, each under different rules. The 1930 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted about 60 million people, most of them Muslim, with Java among the most densely populated places on earth. Malay served as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lingua-franca">lingua franca</a> of administration and commerce, while Chinese communities dominated retail trade and money-lending.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea.jpg" alt="Battle of the Java Sea" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea">Battle of the Java Sea</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haguro-1942.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-netherlands-east-indies-army">Royal Netherlands Indies Army</a> (KNIL), formed in 1830, was a colonial force of mainly Javanese, Ambonese and Manadonese soldiers under European officers, supported by the navy. After the Java War it fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padri-war">Padri War</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-sumatra">West Sumatra</a> until 1837 and then turned to the outer islands, where most of the archipelago still lay beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> control. Expeditions against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a> in 1846, 1848 and 1849, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjarmasin-war">Banjarmasin War</a> from 1859 and campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulawesi">Sulawesi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> extended Dutch power step by step. The longest conflict was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh-war">Aceh War</a>, begun in 1873, which turned into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> struggle; it was largely ended by mobile units of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korps-marechaussee-te-voet">Marechaussee</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-van-heutsz">J. B. van Heutsz</a>, with advice from the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-snouck-hurgronje">Christiaan Snouck Hurgronje</a>, by about 1904, though resistance continued for years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombok">Lombok</a> was conquered in 1894, and in 1906 and 1908 the courts of Badung and Klungkung on Bali chose ritual mass suicide, the <em>puputan</em>, rather than surrender. By about 1910 the whole archipelago, including much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, was under Dutch rule. The colony’s defences were never built for a major war: when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> attacked in 1942, an Allied squadron was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-java-sea">Battle of the Java Sea</a> on 27 February, and the KNIL surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalijati">Kalijati</a> on 8 March 1942.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-van-vollenhoven"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cornelis-van-vollenhoven.jpg" alt="Cornelis van Vollenhoven" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-van-vollenhoven">Cornelis van Vollenhoven</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CornelisvanVollenhoven.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown photographer, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A governor-general, residing at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Buitenzorg</a> (Bogor), ruled the colony on behalf of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> crown under constitutional regulations such as the Government Regulation of 1854. On <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, rule was dual: Dutch residents and controllers of the European civil service supervised a hierarchy of Javanese regents (<em>bupati</em>) drawn from the aristocratic <em>priyayi</em> class, who kept their titles and status while carrying out colonial policy. The sultanates of Yogyakarta and Surakarta survived as autonomous principalities, and in the outer islands hundreds of rulers signed the ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korte-verklaring">Short Declaration</a>’ (<em>Korte Verklaring</em>), accepting Dutch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in return for local self-government. Law was plural: European codes applied to Europeans, while for Indonesians customary law (<em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a></em>), studied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-van-vollenhoven">Cornelis van Vollenhoven</a> and his school at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leiden">Leiden</a>, was recognised alongside Islamic law in family matters. In 1901 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmina-of-the-netherlands">Queen Wilhelmina</a> announced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethical-policy">Ethical Policy</a>, promising welfare through irrigation, education and emigration from crowded Java. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volksraad-dutch-east-indies">People’s Council</a> (<em>Volksraad</em>), first meeting in 1918, gave Indonesians a limited advisory voice. Governor-generals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-van-heutsz">van Heutsz</a> (1904–1909) combined military pacification with administrative reform, but political parties that demanded independence were repressed, and leaders including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta">Mohammad Hatta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutan-sjahrir">Sutan Sjahrir</a> were exiled to remote islands or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boven-digoel">Boven-Digoel</a> camp in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a> in the 1930s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bogor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bogor.jpg" alt="Bogor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Bogor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Istana_presiden_republik_Indonesia_di_Bogor.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nafisathallah, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> created the territorial frame of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. Many of the archipelago’s sultanates, kingdoms and villages had never belonged to one state before; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> conquest between 1830 and 1910 joined them under a single administration, and the Republic of Indonesia claimed exactly those borders, including in the end western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>. The colony also produced the forces that destroyed it. Western-style schools created a small educated elite, and the first modern organisations followed: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budi-utomo">Budi Utomo</a> in 1908, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarekat-islam">Sarekat Islam</a>, which grew into a mass movement in the 1910s, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-indonesia">Communist Party of the Indies</a> in 1920 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-party">Indonesian Nationalist Party</a> in 1927. At the Youth Congress of 1928 young nationalists pledged allegiance to one motherland, one nation and one language, Indonesian, developed from the Malay used in colonial administration. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> the colony was a major source of wealth, and its remittances under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultivation-system">Cultivation System</a> helped restore Dutch public finances in the mid-nineteenth century. <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/max-havelaar">Max Havelaar</a></em> became a classic of Dutch literature and a reference point for later criticism of colonial exploitation. Scientific institutions such as the botanical garden at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogor">Buitenzorg</a>, founded in 1817, the study of <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adat">adat</a></em> law and the Eurasian (Indo) communities that later settled in the Netherlands are further legacies.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sukarno"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sukarno.jpg" alt="Sukarno" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Presiden_Sukarno.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Onbekend/Anonymous, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Colonial authority appeared secure in the 1930s, when the repression of the communist uprisings of 1926–1927 and the exile of nationalist leaders kept open opposition in check, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> cut export earnings sharply and the German occupation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in May 1940 left the colony isolated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> invaded in early 1942 and within weeks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> surrendered. The Japanese interned Dutch civilians, mobilised Indonesians for labour and military training, and promoted nationalist leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a>. Two days after Japan’s surrender, on 17 August 1945, Sukarno and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-hatta">Hatta</a> proclaimed the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. The Netherlands, returning with British help, sought to restore its rule, and four years of war and diplomacy followed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linggadjati-agreement">Linggadjati Agreement</a> of 1946 recognised the Republic’s authority on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, but Dutch ‘police actions’ in July 1947 and December 1948 brought large areas back under Dutch control, including the Republic’s capital at Yogyakarta. International pressure, above all from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, and continuing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> resistance forced a settlement. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-indonesian-round-table-conference">Round Table Conference</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, the Netherlands transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> to the Republic of the United States of Indonesia on 27 December 1949. Only <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/netherlands-new-guinea">Netherlands New Guinea</a> remained Dutch, until it passed to United Nations administration in 1962 and to Indonesia in 1963.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a> turned a scattered trading empire into a colonial state covering the whole Indonesian archipelago, enriching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultivation-system">Cultivation System</a> and plantation exports, and in uniting hundreds of societies under one administration it drew the borders and bred the nationalism of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a>.</em></p>

